\section*{Broader Impact Statement}

As world-model reinforcement learning moves toward real robotic systems with variable-rate sensing and multi-timescale control, silent training failures pose safety risks: an agent that appears to learn may harbor systematically overoptimistic value estimates that only manifest during deployment. This work contributes two practical safeguards. First, the NVE diagnostic ($\geq 2.0\times$, AUROC${=}1.000$ at $n{=}36$) enables practitioners to detect CIVO during training rather than after deployment. Second, the frozen-embedding diagnostic provides a low-cost preliminary check (zero additional compute) that eliminates CIVO entirely (0/16 frozen or removed controls), reducing wasted computation on silently failing runs. Both tools are architecture-specific to DreamerV3's RSSM; validating their transfer to other imagination-based architectures is necessary before broader adoption.

We note two limitations. First, our online NVE detector is a proof-of-concept with wide confidence intervals (TPR 95\% CI [47.8\%, 100\%], $n=5$); premature reliance on it could lead to unnecessary interventions on healthy training runs. Second, all experiments are conducted in simulated environments (DeepMind Control Suite and ManiSkill3) and do not involve real-world robot deployment, physical safety risks, or human subjects. Extending these findings to real-world settings would require additional validation of both the failure mode and the diagnostic tools.
